  "live_records": {
    "q-97770": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97770",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x}}\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x & =-({x}^{2}+4x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-((x+2)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =4-(x+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{4-(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2(x+2)\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+2}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+2}{2})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+2}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+2}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+2}{2})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+2}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
