    "q-97777": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97777",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-6x & =-({x}^{2}+6x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =-((x+3)^{2}-9) \\\\  & =9-(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{9-{(x+3)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{9-9{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+3}{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
