      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+27}}\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+27 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}+27 \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+27}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|x-5|\\sqrt{2+(x-5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}}|\\sqrt{2+2{(\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
